- `domain/entity` — TypeORM entity classes. `User.role` is the enum `admin` | `employee` on `user.entity.ts`. The table name is `auth_users`, because `public.users` already exists for another role set.
- `domain` — ports (`UsersRepository`, `PasswordHasher`, `TokenService`). Each port is an `abstract class` so Nest can inject it. A service depends on the port, not on TypeORM, bcrypt, or JWT.
- `application` — `@Injectable()` use-case services. They import `domain` only.
- `infrastructure` — TypeORM repository, bcrypt hasher, and JWT token service. The repository is the only class that injects TypeORM `Repository<Entity>`.
- `presentation` — thin controllers, feature modules, and `dto/` classes with `class-validator`.
- The feature module lives in `presentation/` and is the only file that imports infrastructure. `TypeOrmModule.forFeature` stays on that module. Do not register entities again in `TypeOrmModule.forRoot`.
- Users: `POST /users`, `GET /users`, `GET /users/:id`. No update or delete. Responses never include `password`.
- Auth: `POST /auth/login` and `POST /auth/refresh` only. Login checks the hashed password and returns an access token plus a refresh token.
- Policies: `POST /policies` (multipart field `document`, PDF or DOCX only), `GET /policies`, `GET /policies/options`, `GET /policies/:id`, `DELETE /policies/:id`. No update. `name`, `version`, and `language` are unique together. `type` is `HEALTH` | `MOTOR` | `PROPERTY`. `language` is `ar` | `en`. After a successful upload the policy `status` is `UPLOADED`. Indexation then moves it through `PROCESSING` to `INDEXED` or `FAILED`, and `currentStage`, `errorCode`, and `errorMessage` record the latest stage.
- Retrieval: `POST /rag/retrieve` only. It returns ranked chunks and citations, or a refusal when evidence is weak. It does not generate an answer. `policyId`, `policyVersionId`, and `documentId` all filter `policy_chunks.policy_id`. `language` filters only when it is `ar` or `en`.
- Claims: `POST /claims`, `GET /claims/types`, `GET /claims`, `GET /claims/:id`, `PATCH /claims/:id`. These routes require `Authorization: Bearer <accessToken>`. A new claim is `SUBMITTED`. The client cannot set `status`, `createdBy`, or `claimNumber`. `admin` can read and update every claim. `employee` can read and update only claims they created. Deleting a policy that still has claims returns `409` `POLICY_HAS_CLAIMS`.
- Analysis: `POST /claims/:id/analyze` and `GET /claims/:id/analysis` use the same access token and the same admin/employee rule. The incident date selects one policy row with the same name and language. Retrieval stays in `RagService`. Payout is calculated in code. `AI_BASE_URL`, `AI_API_KEY`, and `AI_MODEL` configure the model call. A completed analysis opens a `PENDING` approval and sets the claim to `UNDER_REVIEW`.
- Approvals: `GET /approvals`, `GET /approvals/:id`, `POST /approvals/:id/approve`, `POST /approvals/:id/reject`, `POST /approvals/:id/edit-and-approve`. Admin only. Reject requires a comment. Edited payout must not exceed `min(claimedAmount, coverageLimit)`. AI recommendation is preserved; final decision and payout are audited. Claim becomes `APPROVED` or `REJECTED`.

```typescript
import { Module } from '@nestjs/common';
import { TypeOrmModule } from '@nestjs/typeorm';
import { User } from '../domain/entity/user.entity.js';
import { PasswordHasher } from '../domain/password-hasher.js';
import { UsersRepository } from '../domain/users.repository.js';
import { UsersService } from '../application/users.service.js';
import { BcryptPasswordHasher } from '../infrastructure/bcrypt-password.hasher.js';
import { UsersTypeOrmRepository } from '../infrastructure/users.typeorm-repository.js';
import { UsersController } from './users.controller.js';

@Module({
  imports: [TypeOrmModule.forFeature([User])],
  controllers: [UsersController],
  providers: [
    UsersService,
    { provide: UsersRepository, useClass: UsersTypeOrmRepository },
    { provide: PasswordHasher, useClass: BcryptPasswordHasher },
  ],
  exports: [UsersRepository, PasswordHasher],
})
export class UsersModule {}
```

`AuthModule` imports `UsersModule` and uses the exported `UsersRepository` and `PasswordHasher`. It does not import the TypeORM repository class.

## Conventions

- Controllers stay thin. Business logic lives in `application` services. Data access lives in `infrastructure` behind the `domain` repository port.
- Entities are TypeORM classes. Rely on `autoLoadEntities`; do not list them again in `TypeOrmModule.forRoot`.
- Type request bodies as DTO classes with `class-validator` decorators so the global pipe can validate them.
- Read config from `process.env`. Names in use: `PORT`, `NODE_ENV`, `ONLINE_DATABASE_URL`, `CORS_ORIGIN`, `JWT_SECRET`, `JWT_EXPIRES_IN`, `JWT_REFRESH_SECRET`, `JWT_REFRESH_EXPIRES_IN`, `CHUNK_MAX_TOKENS`, `CHUNK_OVERLAP_TOKENS`, `RAG_TOP_K`, `RAG_DENSE_TOP_K`, `RAG_KEYWORD_TOP_K`, `RAG_FINAL_TOP_K`, `RAG_MIN_SCORE`, `RAG_RERANK_ENABLED`, `AI_BASE_URL`, `AI_API_KEY`, `AI_MODEL`.
- CORS is already configured in `main.ts`. Access tokens use `JWT_SECRET` and `JWT_EXPIRES_IN`. Refresh tokens use `JWT_REFRESH_SECRET` and `JWT_REFRESH_EXPIRES_IN`. The access-token guard is applied on claims routes only.
- Put uploaded files in `uploads/`. They are publicly served under `/uploads/`. Use `files` from `src/infrastructure/files.ts` for Multer upload, name, url, add, remove, and replace. Do not write upload handling in a controller.
- `synchronize: true` is for local/dev schema sync. Do not treat it as a production migration strategy.
- When a module's endpoints are finished, add them to the Postman files in `postman/`. One collection per module, named `<module>.postman_collection.json`, with a folder of the same name. Requests use `{{baseUrl}}` and the DTO body. Auth is already in `postman/auth.postman_collection.json`.
